---
title: "Layout: escaping the text column"
---

The reading measure is deliberately narrow, around 70 characters, because that is what
long-form prose wants. Some content does not: a wide table, a multi-panel plot, a
diagram whose labels only fit at size. `.column-margin` already sends a note *out* to
the gutter; `.column-page` sends a block *wider* instead, and they are the same axis.

::: {.column-margin}
This margin note is the narrow end of the same axis the two escapes below widen along.
It shares a page with them on purpose: a document usually wants both.
:::

A footnote reaches the same gutter without an explicit container: `[^label]` puts its
note in the margin[^margin] beside the line that cites it, so the reader never leaves
the sentence they are on. Two notes on one paragraph[^second] stack in the margin in
document order rather than overlapping, because they float rather than being positioned
— and they share that column with the `.column-margin` div above, so the two cannot
collide. Citing the same note twice[^margin] adds a second marker and no second copy.

[^margin]: Notes are for asides that would break the sentence, so they stay short: the
margin column is narrow on purpose.

[^second]: The second note on this paragraph, here to pin that two of them stack instead
of landing on top of each other.

## `.column-page`, past the text column {#sec-page}

`::: {.column-page}` widens a block up to the page width. The prose around it stays at
the reading measure, so the escape reads as a deliberate exception rather than as a
change of layout.

::: {.column-page}
| Track | What sits in it | Width |
| --- | --- | --- |
| gutter | nothing; it is the page edge | at least 1rem, then whatever is left over |
| left side band | the left part of the band | half of what the band has over the measure |
| text | prose, and everything that did not ask to escape | the reading measure |
| gutter + margin column | a footnote or a `.column-margin` aside | 3em, then 16em, and only on a page that has one |
| right side band | the right part of the band, where no note has claimed it | the other half |
| gutter | nothing | at least 1rem |
:::

That table is the reason the feature is one grid rather than three copies of an
arithmetic. A block does not compute a width: it names the column it belongs to, and the
grid resolves what that means in whatever container the page happens to be. The same seven
tracks serve a single document, a website page, a book chapter and a page with a
table-of-contents rail.

**The band is a ceiling, not a size.** A block takes only the width its content needs: a
code box is as wide as its longest line, floored at the reading measure so a two-line
snippet still lines up with the prose, and capped at 100 columns. Where it sits depends
only on whether the page reserved a margin column. Most pages do not, so nothing owns the
right band and every box centres on the prose, sharing one axis with it however much their
widths differ. This page *does* have a note, so here the right band is the note's and the
boxes are right-aligned to the prose instead, sharing that edge. Either way one edge or
one axis is common, and a block never runs under a note.

A table-of-contents rail needs no rule at all. It is a column of the *outer* grid, so it
cannot be reached from inside this one however wide the band grows.

## It is a plain class {#sec-plain}

It is not a new block type, so it needs no `:::` container of its own when the thing being
widened is already a block. It applies to a table, a figure, a `pre`, or a div alike, and
the block keeps its own identity, which is what lets a cross-reference still resolve
through one. Most of what wants the room does not need the class either: a code block, a
folded cell, a numbered listing, a cell's output, a data table and a generated diagram all
take the band on their own, because the measure fits 55 columns of code and PEP 8 asks for
79.

::: {.column-page}
```python
# A listing wide enough to want the room, inside an escape.
records = [(name, kind, centre, offset) for name, kind, centre, offset in containers]
```
:::

Back at the reading measure, and the paragraph after an escape sits exactly where the
paragraph before it did.
